  "solution": {
    "text": "<blockquote><p>Force due to magnetic field on a current carrying conductor is given by,&nbsp;<math xmlns=\"http://www.w3.org/1998/Math/MathML\"><mover><mi>F</mi><mo>&#8594;</mo></mover><mo>=</mo><mi>i</mi><mover><mi>l</mi><mo>&#8594;</mo></mover><mo>&#215;</mo><mover><mi>B</mi><mo>&#8594;</mo></mover></math>.</p><p>The angle between the magnetic field and the direction of the current in the wire&nbsp;<math xmlns=\"http://www.w3.org/1998/Math/MathML\"><mi>C</mi><mi>D</mi></math>&nbsp;is&nbsp;<math xmlns=\"http://www.w3.org/1998/Math/MathML\"><mn>60</mn><mo>&#176;</mo></math>. Therefore,</p><p><math xmlns=\"http://www.w3.org/1998/Math/MathML\"><mi>F</mi><mo>=</mo><mi>i</mi><mi>l</mi><mi>B</mi><mi>sin</mi><mn>60</mn><mo>&#176;</mo><mspace linebreak=\"newline\"></mspace><mo>&#8658;</mo><mi>F</mi><mo>=</mo><mn>10</mn><mo>&#215;</mo><mn>0</mn><mo>.</mo><mn>05</mn><mo>&#215;</mo><mn>0</mn><mo>.</mo><mn>5</mn><mo>&#215;</mo><mfrac><msqrt><mn>3</mn></msqrt><mn>2</mn></mfrac><mspace linebreak=\"newline\"></mspace><mo>&#8658;</mo><mi>F</mi><mo>=</mo><mn>0</mn><mo>.</mo><mn>216</mn><mo>&#160;</mo><mi mathvariant=\"normal\">N</mi></math></p></blockquote>",
